    "content": "<h2>5 kW solar quotes: compare complete cost before payback</h2>\n<p>There is no verified national 5 kW price table on this page. Equipment, roof works, taxes, protection, installation quality and connection scope vary. Obtain dated written quotes instead of treating an old per-watt range as a current offer.</p>\n<h2>Put comparable scope beside each price</h2>\n<p>Ask for panel and inverter model numbers, mounting and structural work, wiring, protection, earthing, monitoring, labour, commissioning, warranties and after-sales responsibility. Confirm which taxes and approval/connection costs are already included. In the planner, put the complete PV installation in the quote field and only <strong>additional</strong> connection/battery amounts in their separate fields. Otherwise the comparison double-counts costs.</p>\n<h2>Check use before choosing 5 kW</h2>\n<p>The label 5 kW describes capacity, not monthly yield or backup. Test measured consumption and a site-specific yield estimate. A daytime-occupied home and a home empty until evening may value the same generation very differently. The self-use percentage is an assumption until supported by interval data. Test a lower self-use scenario and ask whether a smaller quote would cover the useful daytime load more economically.</p>\n<p>For a worked synthetic 5 kW calculation and all formulas, use the <a href=\"/blog/decision-support/solar-planner-pakistan\">main solar guide</a>. Do not use the example cost as a market quote. Ask the installer to confirm roof suitability, sanctioned load and applicable connection requirements before paying a deposit.</p>\n<h2>Before using the estimate</h2>\n<p>Use the <a href=\"/tools/solar-planner\">Solar Planner</a> with measured monthly kWh, a written installed quote, an avoidable import-energy charge and a documented export rate if applicable. Starting numbers are synthetic; they are not market prices, guaranteed yield or a tariff lookup. The form resets on reload. To reuse a scenario, explicitly save one draft on this device, then reopen it in the same browser profile. You can delete it from the planner. Do not enter personal account details.</p>\n<p>The scenario repeats a 30-day month twelve times, with annual generation decline. It separates self-use from export, includes additional connection and battery costs once, deducts annual maintenance and one optional replacement expense, and displays all 30 annual cashflows. Sustained cost recovery is the first end-of-year point that remains recovered through year 30. It can be <strong>not reached</strong>. No financing, discounting, inflation, tax eligibility, future tariff changes, hourly battery dispatch or complete electricity bill is calculated. A roof/electrical design and the applicable agreement need qualified review.</p>\n<h2>Which policy applies? Reviewed 24 November 2026</h2>\n<p>The final Prosumer Regulations took effect on <strong>9 February 2026</strong>. For new net-billing arrangements, imports use the applicable tariff and exports use the national average energy purchase price. Do not substitute a remembered PKR 11 rate or treat export credit as retail-rate savings. The calculator supplies no current numeric export rate. Verify the rate applicable to your connection with the licensee. Regulation 14 permits excess billed credit to carry to the next billing cycle or be paid quarterly; it is not a promise of cash in the same month.</p>\n<p>The <strong>2 April 2026 amendment</strong>, deemed effective from 9 February, preserves the former rate and mechanism for valid agreements executed before commencement until their expiry, subject to its exception for a material modification changing maximum electrical output. Existing-agreement billing is <strong>not modelled</strong> by either calculator mode. Ask the licensee to confirm the agreement, expiry and proposed modification before comparing a replacement or expansion.</p>\n<p>The <strong>28 April fee notification</strong>, also effective from 9 February, sets the Authority concurrence fee at nil for facilities up to 25 kW and PKR 1,000/kW above that threshold. The <strong>6 August amendment</strong> removes Authority concurrence for facilities of 25 kW or below, with approval by the concerned licensee. This does not remove approval, metering or interconnection requirements and costs. Regulations 5 and 13 allow bidirectional measurement using one meter or two. Regulation 12 allows direct procurement if licensee meters are unavailable, subject to licensee testing. Obtain the current written equipment and connection requirements rather than assuming a separate meter or a blanket private-purchase ban.</p>\n<h2>Official sources</h2>\n<p>Policy sources checked 24 November 2026; this is not a market-price refresh. Check your connection and any later changes with the licensee.</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://nepra.org.pk/Legislation/3-Reg/3.35%20NEPRA%20%20Prosumer%20Regulations%202026/NEPRA%20Prosumer%20Regulations%20%28SRO%20251%28I%292026%29%2009-02-26.PDF\">9 February 2026: Prosumer Regulations, SRO 251</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nepra.org.pk/Legislation/3-Reg/3.35%20NEPRA%20%20Prosumer%20Regulations%202026/S.R.O%20547%281%29%202026%20notification%20amendment%20in%20NEPRA%20%28Prosumer%29%20Regulations%2002-04-2026.PDF\">2 April 2026: existing agreements, SRO 547</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nepra.org.pk/Legislation/3-Reg/3.35%20NEPRA%20%20Prosumer%20Regulations%202026/SRO%20709%20-%20Fee%20for%20Prosumers%2028-04-2026.PDF\">28 April 2026: concurrence fee, SRO 709</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nepra.org.pk/Legislation/3-Reg/3.35%20NEPRA%20%20Prosumer%20Regulations%202026/SRO%201330%28I%29-2026%20Dated%2006-08-2026.pdf\">6 August 2026: approval up to 25 kW, SRO 1330</a></li></ul>"
